  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY NIGHT THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 1211 PM MST Wed Nov 25 2026\n\nThe next impactful weather day is Saturday, when the beginnings of a \nlow pressure will sweep down from Wyoming, bringing northwesterly \nwinds to our area. For the Central Mountains, the official forecast \ncurrently calls for 1-3 inches, but given the orographic effects, 3-\n5 inches is now a stronger possibility, especially for Lake \nCounty. Other parts of the CWA below the mountaintops will start\noff as a light rain in the afternoon. As the low pressure moves\nfurther east, and the lower levels respond to a more northerly \nwind flow across the Palmer Divide, the Springs area may end \nwith a light dusting of snow, or even some minor accumulations. \n\nOvernight Monday into Tuesday, a band of 500mb vorticity, \npotentially overlapping with the right entrance region of a 300mb \njet and a shortwave, could give another shot of snow to the \nmountains, particularly the Central Mountains. Wednesday night into \nThursday could similarly give some snow to the Central and San Juan \nMountains, likely being aided by a weak low pressure along the CO/KS \nborder. The warmer than average pattern will make it harder to snow \nin the San Luis Valley, but for now, at least an inch is forecasted \nfor the valley. Models do disagree on weather or not the \nSangres/Wets get snow from this event, but all agree that anyone \nfrom Pueblo into the Eastern Plains will have to continue waiting \nfor their first snowfall for the foreseeable future. Portions of the \nEastern Plains could experience temperatures 10 degF above average \nlater next week.",
